'''astraught†''' (as-trât′), ''p. a.'' [Substituted for ''distraught'', q. v.] Distracted; distraught; aghast. ''Golding.''

'''astraunge†''' (as-trânj′), ''v. t.'' An old form of ''estrange''.

'''astray†''' (a-strā′), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''astraien'', only in pp. ''astraied'' (after OF. ''estraié'', ''estrayé'', whence also appar. the ME. adj.: see ''astray'', ''a.''), or by apheresis ''straien'' (> E. ''stray''), < OF. ''estraier'', stray, prob. = Pr. ''estraguar'', < late ML. ''extravagare'', < L. ''extra'', without, out, + ''vagare'', wander: see ''extravagant''. See ''estray'' and ''stray'', which are doublets of ''astray''.] To go out of the right way; go astray; stray.

'''astray''' (ạ-strā′), ''adv.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''astray'', ''astraie'', ''astraye'' (also, and earlier in recorded date, by expansion and adaptation, ''o strai'', ''on stray'', ''on the straye''; mod. E. as if ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''stray''), also ''astrayey'', < OF. ''estraié'', ''estrayé'', strayed (cf. ME. ''astraied''), pp. of ''estraier'', ''estrayer'', go astray: see ''astray'', ''v.'' The word is thus orig. a p. a., later assimilated to the form of a prep. phr. like ''asleep'', etc. Cf. ''alight''<sup>1</sup> and ''aslope''.] Out of the right way or proper place, either literally or figuratively; wandering.
{{smaller block|Thou shalt not see thy brother’s ox or his sheep go ''astray'', and hide thyself from them.
{{right|Deut. xxii. 1.}}
The guides would purposely lead the Castilians ''astray'', and involve them in morasses. {{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 42.}}}}
